# WebMIX @VERSION@

OBS Studio with a browser frontend: the whole interface (preview, scenes,
sources, filters, audio, transitions, outputs, settings, hotkeys, files, remux)
is served by OBS itself and used from any browser, on this machine or across the
network. The preview is rendered with **WebGPU** from frames OBS encodes in
process, and the "OBS did not shut down properly" safe-mode dialog no longer
stops an unattended start.

## What is new

* **Browser-only update system.** WebMIX checks GitHub releases in the
  background on startup and presents updates exclusively in the browser
  interface — never opening a desktop prompt on the host machine. The update
  dialog renders the release changelog with **Update Now**, **Remind me Later**
  (leaving an **Update X.Y.Z** badge in the menu bar), and **Skip Version**.
* **Verified self-update and automatic restart.** Release packages pre-download
  in the background while outputs are idle, verify their SHA-256 checksum
  against `SHA256SUMS`, install in place (`.zip` on Windows, `.deb` on
  Debian/Ubuntu, `.pkg.tar.zst` on Arch Linux, asking for the `sudo` password in
  the browser when required), and automatically restart WebMIX on the same
  `--web-host` and `--web-port` (`--no-browser`). Open browser tabs wait for the
  new instance to come up and reload into the updated version automatically.
* **Help > Check For Updates & startup setting.** Trigger a check manually at
  any time from **Help > Check For Updates**, or control automatic startup
  checks in **Settings > General > Automatically check for updates on startup**
  (`[General] WebAutoCheckUpdates` in `global.ini`).

The previous release (0.8.0) brought browser-only audio monitoring and automatic
stream start on launch.

## Downloads

| Platform | File | Install |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Windows 10/11 (x64) | `webmix-@VERSION@-windows-x64.zip` | Unpack anywhere and run `bin/64bit/obs64.exe` — it opens the browser interface. Add `--no-web` for the normal desktop UI. |
| Debian / Ubuntu | `webmix_@VERSION@_amd64.deb` | `sudo apt install ./webmix_@VERSION@_amd64.deb` |
| Debug symbols (Linux) | `webmix-dbgsym_@VERSION@_amd64.ddeb` | `sudo apt install ./webmix-dbgsym_@VERSION@_amd64.ddeb` |
| Arch Linux | `webmix-@VERSION@-1-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst` | `sudo pacman -U webmix-@VERSION@-1-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst` |
| Arch Linux (repository) | `webmix.db`, `webmix.files`, `add-repo.sh` | `./add-repo.sh --install` |

Every asset is listed in `SHA256SUMS`.

### Arch Linux repository

The release doubles as a pacman repository, so `pacman -Syu` keeps WebMIX
up to date:

```bash
curl -LO https://github.com/fursyt12/WebMIX/releases/latest/download/add-repo.sh
chmod +x add-repo.sh
./add-repo.sh --install          # adds [webmix] to /etc/pacman.conf and installs
```

The repository is **not signed** (`SigLevel = Optional TrustAll`), because the
packages are built by GitHub Actions without a maintainer key. If that matters
to you, verify the `SHA256SUMS` from this release, or build the package yourself
with `packaging/arch/PKGBUILD`.

## Running it

Web mode is what a plain launch does, on every platform — double-clicking
`obs64.exe`, starting the packaged launcher, or running `obs` from a terminal
all serve the interface and open it in the default browser:

```bash
obs                           # asks once, then opens the interface
webmix                        # same thing; webmix is a symlink to obs
obs --web-host 0.0.0.0        # reachable from the LAN (skips the question)
obs --web-choose              # ask again even if the answer was remembered
obs --no-browser              # serve, but do not open a browser
obs --no-web                  # the classic OBS window instead
```

To start WebMIX with the computer, tick *Start WebMIX when I sign in to this
computer* in **Settings > General**; *Don't open the browser when OBS starts*
and *Start streaming when OBS starts* in the same place apply on every launch.

On a normal launch OBS asks which address and port to serve on — the addresses
are the ones this machine actually has — and then opens the page in the default
browser. Tick *Remember this choice* and later launches start straight away
without asking.

Web mode implies `--disable-missing-files-check`, keeps the window hidden,
creates no tray icon and enables obs-websocket so other clients can connect too.
The remembered address and startup behaviour live in `global.ini` under
`[General]` (`WebAskOnStartup`, `WebHost`, `WebPort`, `WebOpenBrowser`,
`WebStartStreaming`, `WebAutoCheckUpdates`, `WebSkippedUpdateVersion`);
`--startrecording` / `--startstreaming` start an output immediately. OBS's
desktop updater stays off in web mode; instead, WebMIX checks for releases,
shows the changelog, installs the update and restarts itself directly from the
browser (`--disable-updater` turns update checks off completely).

Browsers on other computers sign in with the login from **Tools > Web
Interface Access** (until one is set, only this computer can use the
interface). The connection is plain HTTP and not encrypted, so use it on a
trusted network or behind a reverse proxy with TLS.

## Notes on this build

* The **Browser source** is built and installed (Chromium Embedded Framework is
  bundled, which is where most of the download size comes from). The Lua/Python
  script hosts are still off: rebuild with `-DENABLE_SCRIPTING=ON` to get them
  back.
* The package is named `webmix` and provides/conflicts with `obs-studio`, so it
  can be installed next to (or instead of) your distribution's OBS. It installs
  `/usr/bin/obs` plus a `/usr/bin/webmix` symlink and a WebMIX launcher.
* Still desktop-only: uploading logs to obsproject.com, the Plugin Manager, the
  Auto-Configuration Wizard, undo/redo, grouping sources and importing or
  exporting profiles and scene collections. The Settings dialog covers the same
  pages as before and does not yet look like the desktop one.

Full documentation: <https://github.com/fursyt12/WebMIX/blob/master/web/README.md>
